"""The table's codes for these instances are not at a local optimum of the largest cosine. Local search from the
published coordinates lowered the cosine of 33 of the 35 by more than 1e-12 and of 27 by more than 1e-6, by up to
1.1e-04 (13_122); 33 of them beat the table's displayed (rounded-up) cosine by at least 1e-12. The search per
instance, about eight minutes: sequential linear programming on the near-active pairs (sph.maximize), L-BFGS on the
smooth maximum of the cosines at sharpness rising to 1e9 (sph.descend), and two passes of basin hopping
(sph.improve: shake all points by 1e-4 to 1e-2 of the minimal chord, descend, maximize, keep drops). Where a further
minute of descend and maximize still lowered the cosine by more than 1e-7, or a system sleep cut the run short and
it was rerun (20 of the 35), one-minute passes followed while they gained more than 1e-8, up to four, up to twelve
more on the 1 still moving after those; the longest instance took 17 minutes. It does not converge: a further minute
of descend and maximize still lowers the cosine by up to 8e-08 on 28 of the 35. Instances where nothing beat the
published code keep it. These codes are the bar the task scores against. COS2 is the exact signed squared cosine of
the binary64 points as numerator/denominator, COORDS the points as base64 of little-endian binary64 rows. SCiEL run
spb71 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 33 more, which replace those. SCiEL run spd71 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 31 more,
which replace those. SCiEL run spe71 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 28 more, which replace those. SCiEL run spf71
(Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 34 more, which replace those. SCiEL run spg71 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 34 more, which
replace those."""

